      "problem": "Calculate $\\int (2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
